--TEST--
a granted root reads while a path outside it is refused, including one that reaches outside through `..` — `rule:security/capability-question-is-grant-and-scope`, `rule:config/an-app-block-may-widen-bounded-by-the-global-ceiling`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `Here::path` returns its argument resolved from this file's folder, so the
// paths below are absolute and the `..` built with `Core\Path::join` reaches
// the door as written.
class Here {
    public static function path(#[Core\Path] string $path): string {
        return $path;
    }
}
string $dir = Here::path('.');

// Inside the granted root: this is the whole of what the grant bought.
echo Core\IO::read('./data/note.txt');

// Outside it, and the file exists — so a refusal here is the capability's and
// not the filesystem's. `nvs_runtime::capability::open_read`'s own doc owns
// the ordering that makes that true: the check runs before the open, so a
// path outside the grant is refused whether or not it exists and the two
// messages cannot be used to probe a directory.
try {
    echo Core\IO::read('./secret.txt');
} catch (RuntimeError $error) {
    echo $error->message, "\n";
}

// The same file reached through a `..` segment out of the granted root. A
// literal has its `..` removed while compiling, so this path is joined at run
// time and keeps it. A comparison done on the path as written would accept
// it, which is why the grant is canonicalised before it is compared.
try {
    echo Core\IO::read(Core\Path::join($dir, 'data/../secret.txt'));
} catch (RuntimeError $error) {
    echo $error->message, "\n";
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["./data"]
--FILE data/note.txt--
note
--FILE secret.txt--
secret
--EXPECTF--
note
Core\IO::read needs the capability `fs.read` for %s%esecret.txt, which is not granted
help: grant it in nvs.toml under `[capabilities.fs]`
Core\IO::read needs the capability `fs.read` for %s%edata%e..%esecret.txt, which is not granted
help: grant it in nvs.toml under `[capabilities.fs]`
